<body>
    <h1>Sub-Advisor RVP review</h1>
    <div class=3D"Section1">
        <h2 id=3D"SubAdvisorRVPreview-Purpose"><strong>Purpose</strong></h2=
>
<p>This page consolidates insights from RVP discussions and sub-advisory re=
views to inform the future-state design of:</p>
<ul>
 <li data-uuid=3D"61bf1ad5-e5a2-4a0c-8eb9-f691702dfe5e"><strong>Multi-firm =
structure support</strong></li>
 <li data-uuid=3D"b8a50b97-ac4d-4608-8f4d-cbc72df1e59d"><strong>Sub-advisor=
y, vendor and referral user access models</strong></li>
 <li data-uuid=3D"ae26a0f4-50d9-4672-b479-9765e5d165a3"><strong>Governance =
and controls</strong></li>
</ul>
<p>=E2=9A=A0=EF=B8=8F Current-state challenges are driven by fragmented acc=
ess models, poor data visibility, and evolving firm structures (M&amp;A, ou=
tsourcing, multi-manager models).<br>
  The platform lacks standardization, auditability, and flexibility require=
d to support modern firm operating models.</p>
<p><br></p>
<div class=3D"panel" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
 <div class=3D"panelHeader" style=3D"border-bottom-width: 1px;">
  <b>Insights</b>
 </div>
 <div class=3D"panelContent">
  <h2 id=3D"SubAdvisorRVPreview-Purpose.1"><strong>Purpose</strong></h2>
  <p>This page consolidates RVP discovery insights across firm interactions=
, user access patterns, and relationship structures, organized by relations=
hip types, structural models, operational use cases, and data issues.</p>
  <hr>
  <h2 id=3D"SubAdvisorRVPreview-CoreRelationshipTypes"><strong>Core Relatio=
nship Types</strong></h2>
  <h3 id=3D"SubAdvisorRVPreview-Sub-Advisor"><strong>Sub-Advisor</strong></=
h3>
  <p><strong>Findings</strong></p>
  <ul>
   <li data-uuid=3D"5cdb9e44-4f6a-4e3f-9bfc-73e134663882">Sub-advisory rela=
tionships are inconsistently represented and difficult to identify</li>
   <li data-uuid=3D"724da141-9e16-46d0-ad71-8fc7cc8a8f67">Often identified =
through&nbsp;duplicate user IDs, email patterns, or RR codes rather than ex=
plicit tagging</li>
   <li data-uuid=3D"2c4491d7-4fc8-4aea-8f97-adc6b1e58dfb">Some access exist=
s for historical or operational reasons (e.g., account viewing) rather than=
 active sub-advisory mandates</li>
   <li data-uuid=3D"27d78b99-7f37-4258-9261-234dc0e9b3d5">Growing variance =
in models:=20
    <ul>
     <li>Some firms continue to rely on sub-advisors</li>
     <li>Others are transitioning to direct fund ownership and internal man=
agement</li>
    </ul></li>
   <li data-uuid=3D"70b3ec1f-fe07-4deb-90f7-29528a7a09fc">Limited visibilit=
y into full sub-advisor networks, especially beyond NBIN-held data</li>
  </ul>
  <hr>
  <h3 id=3D"SubAdvisorRVPreview-Multi-Manager/OutsourcedIFM(StructuredSub-A=
dvisor)"><strong>Multi-Manager / Outsourced IFM (Structured Sub-Advisor)</s=
trong></h3>
  <ul>
   <li data-uuid=3D"d3445e19-64de-4160-8ee2-965f9b7fe81c">Increasing use of=
 outsourced IFM providers (e.g., Majestic) across PM firms</li>
   <li data-uuid=3D"734f087a-2f98-463c-a95f-4efb1b18d252">Typical model inc=
ludes:=20
    <ul>
     <li>Separate PM and IFM entities</li>
     <li>Roll-up to a parent firm structure</li>
    </ul></li>
  </ul>
  <p>Some firms operate multi-manager platforms (e.g., Guardian, HighView) =
with multiple sub-advisors</p>
  <ul>
   <li data-uuid=3D"7e5f6a59-d277-467a-af85-e32292f91278">When a sub-adviso=
r (e.g., Parametric, Fiera) works with&nbsp;multiple PM firms:=20
    <ul>
     <li>They are set up separately within each firm=E2=80=99s environment<=
/li>
     <li>They are treated like a user of each individual firm, not as a sha=
red entity</li>
    </ul></li>
  </ul>
  <h3 id=3D"SubAdvisorRVPreview-Consequence"><strong>Consequence</strong></=
h3>
  <ul>
   <li data-uuid=3D"dadb470f-20f7-43bf-9223-1a93936e1ed1">The same sub-advi=
sor:=20
    <ul>
     <li>Has multiple user setups / accounts</li>
     <li>Is managed independently by each firm</li>
    </ul></li>
   <li data-uuid=3D"754d3d1b-13e1-4dee-83b6-0bfeefeab160">This results in:=
=20
    <ul>
     <li>Duplicate access configurations</li>
     <li>Repeated onboarding/offboarding effort</li>
     <li>No single =E2=80=9Csource of truth=E2=80=9D for that sub-advisor</=
li>
    </ul></li>
  </ul>
  <p><br></p>
  <hr>
  <h3 id=3D"SubAdvisorRVPreview-Referral"><strong>Referral</strong></h3>
  <ul>
   <li data-uuid=3D"91bf026d-c6f0-4df8-ab0e-1c196b245f05">Access typically =
includes but is not limited to:=20
    <ul>
     <li>Onboarding capabilities</li>
     <li>View-only access to client accounts</li>
    </ul></li>
   <li data-uuid=3D"6f3207c0-9bd7-4b95-b55f-5785df4dde68">Access is granted=
 to support:=20
    <ul>
     <li>Client onboarding</li>
     <li>Ongoing referral fee validation</li>
     <li data-uuid=3D"97e0c526-46a0-4aa8-a941-241f3b3bcf23">Accounting func=
tions e.g. Tax slips, statements</li>
    </ul></li>
   <li data-uuid=3D"e30f44b6-57a3-4709-b07b-ad4b2f6f45e1">Referral-driven m=
odels are used by&nbsp; firms BCV and Tri-Cert</li>
   <li data-uuid=3D"ffcbdd91-f41f-43a2-9293-443b38b64bea">Referral roles ar=
e not distinguished from other access types</li>
  </ul>
  <hr>
  <h2 id=3D"SubAdvisorRVPreview-Structural/OrganizationalTypes"><strong>Str=
uctural / Organizational Types</strong></h2>
  <h3 id=3D"SubAdvisorRVPreview-Merger&amp;Acquisition(Multi-FirmAccess)"><=
strong>Merger &amp; Acquisition (Multi-Firm Access)</strong></h3>
  <ul>
   <li data-uuid=3D"f43ff652-381e-4070-bcda-27aa13aa1634">Increasing freque=
ncy of M&amp;A activity leading to parent-child firm structures</li>
   <li data-uuid=3D"56a78a28-20e4-473c-b907-797bdb845bd4">Acquired firms ty=
pically remain in separate environments post-acquisition</li>
   <li data-uuid=3D"87086c12-5d58-403e-8de0-297d1187e2c6">Users must mainta=
in multiple IDs across entities</li>
   <li data-uuid=3D"edf79a9b-3ae5-4f17-8d9e-2d5d65a1c9f4">Platform constrai=
nts prevent consolidated access across firms</li>
   <li data-uuid=3D"401e4c9c-a61b-441c-b50e-1e85563117cb">Rebranding is oft=
en used instead of full system migration or integration</li>
   <li data-uuid=3D"927cadde-d442-455b-80b7-f7ce62246573">Examples include =
Croft acquisitions (Venable Park, JC Hood, QCPM)</li>
  </ul>
  <hr>
  <h2 id=3D"SubAdvisorRVPreview-Operational/SupportTypes"><strong>Operation=
al / Support Types</strong></h2>
  <h3 id=3D"SubAdvisorRVPreview-OutsourcedAdmin/Accounting"><strong>Outsour=
ced Admin / Accounting</strong></h3>
  <ul>
   <li data-uuid=3D"ca2001be-455c-4681-9738-8ed3d7ec9164">Some access attri=
buted to fund accountants, compliance or administrative service providers</=
li>
   <li data-uuid=3D"513e9c3c-546f-4e96-b2e1-3ab64bb91946">In certain cases:=
=20
    <ul>
     <li>Users have unexpected system access instead of data-only feeds</li=
>
     <li>Email domains do not align with primary firm entities</li>
    </ul></li>
   <li data-uuid=3D"df33cad6-781a-4837-9a22-ffa24a6d797f">Indicates inconsi=
stent implementation of intended operating model</li>
   <li data-uuid=3D"8249094a-b9c2-4daa-92b6-cabe05e3ce02">Distinction betwe=
en:=20
    <ul>
     <li>Operational support roles</li>
     <li>True advisory relationships is not always clear</li>
    </ul></li>
  </ul>
  <hr>
  <h3 id=3D"SubAdvisorRVPreview-Third-PartyVendorAccess"><strong>Third-Part=
y Vendor Access</strong></h3>
  <ul>
   <li data-uuid=3D"85e395d1-6d38-470c-b317-dd30dcfa0bf0">Presence of third=
-party or external users whose roles are not always clearly defined; appear=
 as sub-advisors but function as administrative or vendor support</li>
   <li data-uuid=3D"d90e36bb-8e66-4aa2-b507-2f97b4110d2b">Limited transpare=
ncy into:=20
    <ul>
     <li>Who the third party represents</li>
     <li>Why access was provisioned</li>
    </ul></li>
   <li data-uuid=3D"0867b32f-a3e9-404a-a6d6-34bbd2443a38">Reflects lack of =
standardized classification of external access types</li>
  </ul>
  <hr>
  <h2 id=3D"SubAdvisorRVPreview-DataIssues(NotTrueRelationships)"><strong>D=
ata Issues (Not True Relationships)</strong></h2>
  <h3 id=3D"SubAdvisorRVPreview-StaleAccess"><strong>Stale Access</strong><=
/h3>
  <ul>
   <li data-uuid=3D"59768751-11cd-41c9-86a5-7e432cdf6af9">Evidence of users=
:=20
    <ul>
     <li>No longer with firms but retaining access</li>
     <li>Maintaining access due to missed or ineffective attestation proces=
ses</li>
    </ul></li>
   <li data-uuid=3D"c4e40199-0d96-4ba8-8943-bf7e56e46e16">Legacy access per=
sists due to:=20
    <ul>
     <li>Manual governance processes</li>
     <li>Lack of regular cleanup and enforcement</li>
    </ul></li>
   <li data-uuid=3D"b95b0d71-c26e-45e8-a616-58f2558c66c9">Significant effor=
t required to:=20
    <ul>
     <li>Validate current access lists</li>
     <li>Remove outdated permissions</li>
    </ul></li>
   <li data-uuid=3D"a8f9aff9-ebdf-4083-8ac8-55719d632fc7">Creates both:=20
    <ul>
     <li>Security and compliance risks</li>
     <li>Noise in identifying true relationship structures</li>
    </ul></li>
  </ul>
  <hr>
  <h2 id=3D"SubAdvisorRVPreview-Recommendedfirmsforvalidationengagements"><=
strong>Recommended firms for validation e</strong><strong>ngagements</stron=
g></h2>
  <ul>
   <li style=3D"list-style-type: none;">
    <ul>
     <li>M&amp;A =E2=86=92 Croft</li>
     <li>Sub-advisory / IFM =E2=86=92 Newport</li>
     <li>Referral agents =E2=86=92 BCV</li>
    </ul></li>
   <li data-uuid=3D"71a936ab-cf16-4ac3-8500-2e168f61433d">
    <p>Additional engagement with:</p>
    <ul>
     <li>Complex firms (e.g., Guardian, HighView)&nbsp;</li>
    </ul></li>
  </ul>
  <p><br></p>
 </div>
</div>
<p><br></p>
<div class=3D"panel" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
 <div class=3D"panelHeader" style=3D"border-bottom-width: 1px;">
  <b>RVP meeting notes</b>
 </div>
 <div class=3D"panelContent">
  <p class=3D"auto-cursor-target"><strong>Meeting 1 - May 27, 2026&nbsp;</s=
trong></p>
  <p>Attendees: Todd Brennand, Penny Lam, Susanne Clifford&nbsp;</p>
  <p>Meeting notes:</p>
  <p><strong>User Access Anomalies :&nbsp;</strong></p>
  <ul>
   <li style=3D"list-style-type: none;" data-uuid=3D"a0c83ca5-fe9a-4c23-956=
c-8fe2c48165ca">
    <ul>
     <li><strong>IPC User Access in Bellwether: </strong>Todd and Susanne i=
dentified an IPC user with access in the Bellwether environment, which seem=
ed incorrect.&nbsp;</li>
     <li><strong>Fund Accountant Access in McIlvain: </strong>Susanne noted=
 a user with two IDs whose email domain did not match Higgins or McIlvain, =
suggesting it might be a fund accountant or administrative services. Todd e=
xplained that fund accountants typically require data feeds rather than acc=
ount access and recommended following up with Tim McIlvain to verify the se=
tup.</li>
     <li><strong>Quarterly Attestation Concerns: </strong>Todd expressed do=
ubts about the thoroughness of quarterly attestations by firms, indicating =
that anomalies in user access might go unnoticed, and emphasized the import=
ance of reviewing these cases to ensure proper platform setups.</li>
     <li><strong>Referral Agent Access: </strong>Susanne and Todd discussed=
 referral agent access, with Todd clarifying that referral agents, such as =
accountants or lawyers, may be granted onboarding or view-only access to fa=
cilitate client onboarding and justify ongoing referral fees, and that risk=
 teams are comfortable with this arrangement.</li>
    </ul></li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>Multi-Firm Relationships and Mergers:&nbsp;</strong></p>
  <ul>
   <li style=3D"list-style-type: none;" data-uuid=3D"7f934fe2-86c4-44d7-b5d=
2-aa5857875dba">
    <ul>
     <li><strong>Croft Aquisition of Venable Park and JC Hood: </strong>Tod=
d explained that Croft Financials' acquisition of Venable Park required sep=
arate user access for each environment due to platform limitations, resulti=
ng in Croft users needing additional IDs to access Venable Park accounts. <=
strong>&nbsp;</strong>Todd noted that Croft's acquisition of JC Hood would =
lead to similar access challenges and predicted more such cases as mergers =
and acquisitions continue to increase among platform firms.</li>
     <li><strong>Platform Rebranding: </strong>Todd described the process o=
f rebranding acquired firm environments, such as Venable Park, rather than =
migrating all accounts, to maintain separate environments and comply with p=
latform constraints.</li>
    </ul></li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>Sub-Advisory Relationships and Outsourcing:&nbsp;</strong></p>
  <ul>
   <li style=3D"list-style-type: none;" data-uuid=3D"e9f60636-0d28-4d73-b20=
6-196ac0d3a513">
    <ul>
     <li><strong>Sub-Advisor Engagement Process: </strong>Todd explained th=
at as firms engage sub-advisors, the implementation and risk teams are invo=
lved to ensure proper oversight, and that sub-advisors are set up as employ=
ees of each PM firm to maintain control and facilitate access removal when =
necessary.</li>
     <li><strong>Parametric Example: </strong>Todd cited Parametric, a larg=
e firm engaged by Northwood and Newport, as an example of sub-advisory rela=
tionships, noting that Parametric prefers direct access but the platform re=
quires them to be managed by each PM firm individually.</li>
     <li><strong>Outsourced IFM Services: </strong>Todd highlighted Majesti=
c as a major provider of outsourced IFM services, with many PMs on the plat=
form delegating IFM responsibilities to Majestic, which aligns with the pla=
tform's structure and access controls.</li>
     <li><strong>Asset Allocator vs. Sub-Advisor: </strong>Todd distinguish=
ed between asset allocators, who select PMs to manage fund sleeves, and PMs=
 who provide sub-advisory services, noting that most traditional PMs prefer=
 to manage assets internally rather than outsource.</li>
    </ul></li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>PM-IFM Relationship Dynamics and Account Structures:&nbsp;</st=
rong></p>
  <ul>
   <li style=3D"list-style-type: none;" data-uuid=3D"41dabb34-800e-427b-bf8=
2-40a6e010a4b6">
    <ul>
     <li><strong>PM Launching Fund and IFM Registration: </strong>Todd desc=
ribed the typical process where a PM grows their business, decides to launc=
h a fund, and obtains IFM registration, resulting in separate PM and IFM ac=
counts that roll up to a parent account in Salesforce.</li>
     <li><strong>Outsourcing IFM to Majestic: </strong>Todd explained that =
some PMs choose to outsource IFM responsibilities to firms like Majestic, a=
llowing them to focus on portfolio management while Majestic handles fund a=
dministration.</li>
     <li><strong>Standalone IFM Use Case: </strong>Todd outlined the rare s=
cenario where a firm acts solely as an IFM, launching funds for distributio=
n without managing private clients, and noted that these firms still requir=
e both PM and IFM registrations.</li>
     <li><strong>Account Range and Segregation: </strong>Todd detailed that=
 fund accounts are typically opened in the 26x prime brokerage account rang=
e for segregation and reporting, with legacy cases like Newport and Croft h=
aving fund accounts within private client ranges, leading to double countin=
g.</li>
    </ul></li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>User Access Configuration and System Limitations:&nbsp;</stron=
g></p>
  <ul>
   <li style=3D"list-style-type: none;" data-uuid=3D"7410857f-bace-4454-880=
0-fd770c27a4cf">
    <ul>
     <li><strong>Multiple User IDs Requirement: </strong>Todd explained tha=
t users often require separate IDs for each firm or account type due to sys=
tem constraints, such as the inability to blend access between ICBMB and IC=
PMC environments, resulting in operational overhead for users.</li>
     <li><strong>Application Inconsistencies: </strong>Penny noted inconsis=
tencies between applications like Compass, NetRep, and SecureZone regarding=
 user access, and emphasized the need to clean up and standardize configura=
tions across the platform.</li>
     <li><strong>Consultation with Implementation Team: </strong>Todd recom=
mended engaging Kayla from the Implementations team to clarify access limit=
ations and explore possible solutions, as well as consulting pilot firms li=
ke Croft and Newport to validate design and controls.</li>
     <li><strong>Rep Code Limitations: </strong>Todd mentioned that BCV, no=
w owned by ATB, faces challenges with the number of rep codes that can be c=
onfigured under one user ID, and suggested leveraging ID1 and ID2 fields to=
 address this issue.</li>
    </ul></li>
  </ul>
