  <p>Meeting 2 - May 27, 2026</p>
  <p>Attendees: Matt Pollock, Penny Lam, Susanne Clifford</p>
  <p>Meeting notes:</p>
  <ul>
   <li data-uuid=3D"2c5f41aa-65b6-4f47-a773-5fb62d754eab"><strong>Review of=
 Sub-Advisory and Referral Relationships:&nbsp;</strong>
    <ul>
     <li><strong>Shift Away from Sub-Advisory: </strong>Matthew explained t=
hat many multifamily office firms, such as Altium, have moved away from sub=
-advisory relationships and now prefer to purchase funds directly, managing=
 them internally rather than relying on sub-advisors.</li>
     <li><strong>Firm-Specific Status Updates: </strong>The team discussed =
the current sub-advisory or referral status for firms including Altium, Cla=
ret, Jaroslovsky, Georgian, BCV, and Kerr, noting which firms still have su=
b-advisory accounts, which have transitioned to direct fund purchases, and =
which may require follow-up to confirm their current practices.</li>
     <li><strong>Access and Account Management: </strong>They addressed the=
 issue of legacy accounts and user access, highlighting that some users lis=
ted as having access no longer work at the firms, and that there is a need =
for better governance and regular cleanup of access rights.</li>
     <li><strong>Purpose of Account Viewing: </strong>Matthew clarified tha=
t in some cases, such as between Kerr and Jaroslovsky, access is provided f=
or account viewing only, often due to the administrative burden of moving a=
ccounts, rather than for active sub-advisory management.</li>
     <li><strong>Compliance and Attestation: </strong>The group noted that =
attestation processes are not always followed or understood by firms, leadi=
ng to outdated access and potential compliance risks, and suggested that fi=
rms should be required to maintain up-to-date disclosures of their sub-advi=
sory and referral relationships.</li>
    </ul></li>
   <li data-uuid=3D"67581e3f-a28f-4320-a086-7f8a97e069bd"><strong>Operation=
al Challenges and Data Management:&nbsp;</strong>
    <ul>
     <li><strong>Visibility and Data Accuracy: </strong>Penny highlighted t=
he difficulty in maintaining visibility into sub-advisory relationships, no=
ting that it took the team three months to compile the current list and tha=
t data is often outdated or incomplete.</li>
     <li><strong>User Access and Risk Management: </strong>The team discuss=
ed the risks associated with user access, including cases where former empl=
oyees retain access to systems, and emphasized the need for regular access =
reviews and a more robust attestation process.</li>
     <li><strong>Digitalization of Processes: </strong>Penny proposed movin=
g attestation and delegation processes to digital platforms to improve effi=
ciency and traceability, suggesting that digital delegation and automated t=
racking would reduce manual errors and oversight.</li>
     <li><strong>Role of Specialty Teams: </strong>Matthew suggested the cr=
eation of a specialty team focused on user access management, given the hig=
h risk and complexity involved in ensuring correct permissions and complian=
ce.</li>
    </ul></li>
   <li data-uuid=3D"ea2383cc-b8e3-4b94-a6fa-8005a343c64d"><strong>Technical=
 and Process Considerations for System Enhancements:&nbsp;</strong>
    <ul>
     <li><strong>System Flexibility and Onboarding: </strong>Penny and Matt=
hew discussed Compass's flexibility in supporting new types of relationship=
s, such as referrals, and the need to define and document these use cases t=
o ensure the system can accommodate them.</li>
     <li><strong>Pilot Testing with Partner Firms: </strong>The team agreed=
 to involve representative firms, particularly those with more complex or u=
nique use cases, in pilot testing system enhancements to validate new proce=
sses before full implementation.</li>
     <li><strong>Integration of Net Rep and Secure Zone: </strong>Susanne m=
entioned that upcoming changes would include integrating net rep and secure=
 zone information, which may affect how access and relationships are manage=
d within the system.</li>
     <li><strong>Surveying Firms for Requirements: </strong>Matthew suggest=
ed conducting a brief survey with firms to gather information on their need=
s and preferences regarding sub-advisory and referral access, to inform sys=
tem design and process improvements.</li>
    </ul></li>
   <li data-uuid=3D"6eb80043-e078-4489-9b4d-8b53a247b8e2"><strong>Action It=
ems and Next Steps:&nbsp;</strong>
    <ul>
     <li><strong>Legacy Access Cleanup: </strong>Matthew and Penny agreed o=
n the need to review and remove outdated sub-advisory access, particularly =
for firms and users no longer active, as part of the ongoing project.</li>
     <li><strong>Documentation and Traceability: </strong>Penny emphasized =
the importance of definitively defining relationship types and ensuring the=
y are properly documented in systems like Firm 360 for future traceability.=
</li>
     <li><strong>Timeline for Implementation: </strong>The group acknowledg=
ed that the solution would take four to five months to implement, with a fi=
nal review and onboarding of relationships into the new system at the end o=
f the process.</li>
     <li><strong>Outreach and Feedback Collection: </strong>They planned to=
 reach out to selected partner firms through Matthew and the RVPs to gather=
 feedback and validate new processes, with the possibility of using surveys=
 to streamline input collection.</li>
    </ul></li>
  </ul>
